      "ch": "Joshua 18",
      "verses": [
        "The whole community of Israel gathered at Shiloh and set up the tent of meeting there. The land was now under their control.",
        "But seven of Israel’s tribes had still not received their inheritance.",
        "So Joshua said to the Israelites, “How long will you wait before you go and take over the land that the Lord, the God of your fathers, has given you?”",
        "Choose three men from each tribe, and I will send them out. They will go through the land and write a description of it, showing what each tribe will inherit. Then they will come back to me.",
        "They will divide the land into seven parts. Judah will stay in its land in the south, and the house of Joseph will stay in its land in the north.",
        "Write a description of the land divided into seven parts and bring it here to me. Then I will cast lots for you here in front of the Lord our God.",
        "But the Levites get no share of land among you, because serving the Lord as priests is their inheritance. And Gad, Reuben, and half the tribe of Manasseh have already received their inheritance east of the Jordan. Moses the servant of the Lord gave it to them.",
        "So the men got ready to leave. Joshua gave orders to those who were going to describe the land: “Go and walk all through the land and write a description of it. Then come back to me, and I will cast lots for you here in front of the Lord at Shiloh.”",
        "The men went all through the land and wrote a description of it on a scroll, town by town, in seven parts. Then they came back to Joshua at the camp at Shiloh.",
        "Joshua cast lots for them in front of the Lord at Shiloh. There he divided the land among the Israelites, giving each group its share.",
        "The lot came up for the tribe of Benjamin, family by family. The land they received lay between the people of Judah and the people of Joseph.",
        "On the north side, their border started at the Jordan. It went up the slope north of Jericho, climbed west through the hill country, and came out at the wilderness of Beth-aven.",
        "From there the border crossed over to the slope south of Luz, which is Beth-el. Then it went down to Ataroth-adar, near the hill south of Lower Beth-horon.",
        "From there the border turned south along the west side, from the hill facing Beth-horon on the south. It ended at Kirjath-baal, which is Kirjath-jearim, a town of the people of Judah. This was the western side.",
        "The southern side began at the edge of Kirjath-jearim. The border went west and reached the spring of the waters of Nephtoah.",
        "Then the border went down to the foot of the hill that faces the valley of the son of Hinnom, at the north end of the valley of the giants. It went down the valley of Hinnom, along the south slope of the Jebusite city, and down to En-rogel.",
        "It curved north and went on to En-shemesh, then toward Geliloth, which faces the pass going up to Adummim. Then it went down to the stone of Bohan son of Reuben.",
        "It passed along the slope north of the Arabah (the Jordan Valley) and went down into the Arabah.",
        "Next the border passed along the slope north of Beth-hoglah and ended at the north bay of the Salt Sea, where the Jordan flows in at the south. This was the southern border.",
        "The Jordan was the border on the east side. These were the borders all around the land that Benjamin’s descendants inherited, family by family.",
        "These were the towns of the tribe of Benjamin, family by family: Jericho, Beth-hoglah, the valley of Keziz,",
        "Beth-arabah, Zemaraim, Beth-el,",
        "Avim, Parah, Ophrah,",
        "Chephar-haammonai, Ophni, and Gaba. That made twelve towns, with their villages.",
        "Gibeon, Ramah, Beeroth,",
        "Mizpeh, Chephirah, Mozah,",
        "Rekem, Irpeel, Taralah,",
        "Zelah, Eleph, Jebusi (which is Jerusalem), Gibeath, and Kirjath. That made fourteen towns, with their villages. This was the inheritance of Benjamin’s descendants, family by family."
      ],
