        "These are the nations the Lord left in the land to test Israel by them, all the Israelites who had not been through any of the wars in Canaan.",
        "He did this only so that the later generations of Israel would know war and be taught how to fight, at least those who had never known it before.",
        "These were the five rulers of the Philistines, all the Canaanites, the Sidonians, and the Hivites who lived in the mountains of Lebanon, from Mount Baal-hermon to where the road goes into Hamath.",
        "They were there to test Israel, to find out whether the people would obey the Lord’s commandments, which He had given their fathers through Moses.",
        "So the Israelites lived among the Canaanites, Hittites, Amorites, Perizzites, Hivites, and Jebusites.",
        "They married the daughters of these nations, gave their own daughters to their sons, and worshipped their gods.",
        "The Israelites did what was evil in the Lord’s eyes. They forgot the Lord their God and worshipped Baalim (the Baals, false gods) and the Asherah poles (wooden poles set up for a false goddess).",
        "So the Lord’s anger burned against Israel, and He handed them over to Chushan-rishathaim, king of Mesopotamia. The Israelites served Chushan-rishathaim for eight years.",
        "But when the Israelites cried out to the Lord, the Lord raised up someone to rescue them: Othniel son of Kenaz, the younger brother of Caleb, and he saved them.",
        "The Spirit of the Lord came on him, and he became Israel’s judge and went out to fight. The Lord gave Chushan-rishathaim king of Mesopotamia into his power, and Othniel won against Chushan-rishathaim.",
        "Then the land had peace for forty years, and Othniel son of Kenaz died.",
        "Once more the Israelites did what was evil in the Lord’s eyes. So the Lord made Eglon king of Moab strong against Israel, because they had done evil in the Lord’s eyes.",
        "Eglon joined with the Ammonites and the Amalekites. He went and attacked Israel, and they took over the city of palm trees (Jericho).",
        "The Israelites served Eglon king of Moab for eighteen years.",
        "Then the Israelites cried out to the Lord, and the Lord raised up someone to rescue them: Ehud son of Gera, from the tribe of Benjamin, a left-handed man. The Israelites sent him to take their tribute (a payment owed to a ruling king) to Eglon king of Moab.",
        "Ehud had made himself a double-edged dagger about a foot and a half long. He strapped it on his right thigh under his clothes.",
        "He brought the tribute to Eglon king of Moab. Now Eglon was a very fat man.",
        "After Ehud finished handing over the tribute, he sent away the men who had carried it.",
        "But when he reached the carved idols near Gilgal, he turned back by himself and said, “I have a secret message for you, my king.” The king said, “Quiet!” and all the servants standing around him went out.",
        "Ehud came to him while he sat all alone in his cool room upstairs. Ehud said, “I have a message from God for you.” The king got up from his seat.",
        "Ehud reached out with his left hand, pulled the dagger from his right thigh, and drove it into the king’s belly.",
        "Even the handle went in after the blade, and the fat closed over the blade. Ehud did not pull the dagger out of his belly, and the waste came out.",
        "Then Ehud went out through the porch. He shut the doors of the upper room behind him and locked them.",
        "After Ehud left, the king’s servants came. When they saw that the doors of the upper room were locked, they said, “He must be covering his feet (relieving himself) in the cool room.”",
        "They waited until they were embarrassed, but he still did not open the doors of the upper room. So they took a key and opened them, and there was their master, fallen on the floor, dead.",
        "While they waited, Ehud got away. He went past the carved idols and escaped to Seirath.",
        "When he got there, he blew a ram’s horn trumpet in the hill country of Ephraim. The Israelites came down from the hills with him, and he led the way.",
        "He told them, “Follow me, for the Lord has given your enemies the Moabites into your power.” So they went down after him and took control of the river crossings of the Jordan that led to Moab, and they let no one cross.",
        "At that time they killed about ten thousand men of Moab, all of them strong, brave fighters. Not one man escaped.",
        "So that day Moab was brought under Israel’s power, and the land had peace for eighty years.",
        "After Ehud came Shamgar son of Anath. He killed six hundred Philistines with an ox goad (a long pointed stick for driving cattle). He also rescued Israel."
