        "When David got up the next morning, the word of the Lord had come to the prophet Gad, David’s seer:",
        "“Go and tell David, ‘This is what the Lord says: I am giving you three choices. Choose one of them, and I will do it to you.’”",
        "So Gad went to David and told him, “Should seven years of famine come on your land? Or should you run for three months from your enemies while they chase you? Or should there be three days of deadly disease in your land? Now think it over and decide what answer I should take back to the One who sent me.”",
        "David told Gad, “I am in a terrible spot. Please let us fall into the hands of the Lord, because His mercy is great. But don’t let me fall into the hands of men.”",
        "So the Lord sent a deadly disease on Israel from that morning until the appointed time. From Dan to Beer-sheba, seventy thousand of the people died.",
        "When the angel reached out his hand over Jerusalem to destroy it, the Lord felt sorrow over the disaster. He said to the angel who was destroying the people, “That’s enough! Pull back your hand now.” The angel of the Lord was then by the threshing floor of Araunah the Jebusite (a flat place where grain was separated from straw).",
        "When David saw the angel who was striking down the people, he said to the Lord, “Look, I am the one who sinned. I am the one who did wrong. But these sheep, what have they done? Please let Your hand come down on me and on my father’s family.”",
        "That same day Gad came to David and told him, “Go up and build an altar to the Lord on the threshing floor of Araunah the Jebusite.”",
        "So David went up, as Gad had told him and as the Lord had commanded.",
        "Araunah looked out and saw the king and his servants coming toward him. So Araunah went out and bowed down before the king with his face to the ground.",
        "Araunah asked, “Why has my lord the king come to see me, his servant?” David answered, “To buy your threshing floor so I can build an altar to the Lord. Then the plague on the people will stop.”",
        "Araunah told David, “Let my lord the king take whatever he wants and offer it up. Look, here are oxen for the burnt offering, and the threshing sledges and the oxen’s yokes can be used for firewood.”",
        "Araunah, like a king, gave all of this to the king. And Araunah said to the king, “May the Lord your God accept you.”",
        "But the king told Araunah, “No, I insist on paying you for it. I will not offer burnt offerings to the Lord my God that cost me nothing.” So David bought the threshing floor and the oxen for fifty shekels of silver.",
