      "ch": "2 Kings 18",
      "verses": [
        "In the third year of Hoshea son of Elah king of Israel, Hezekiah son of Ahaz king of Judah became king.",
        "He was twenty-five when he became king, and he ruled in Jerusalem for twenty-nine years. His mother was Abi, the daughter of Zachariah.",
        "He did what the Lord saw as right, just as his forefather David had done in everything.",
        "He got rid of the high places (hilltop shrines), smashed the sacred stone pillars, and cut down the Asherah pole. He broke into pieces the bronze serpent Moses had made, because until then the people of Israel had been burning incense to it. He called it Nehushtan (a name that sounds like the Hebrew word for bronze).",
        "Hezekiah trusted in the Lord, the God of Israel. None of the kings of Judah after him was like him, and none before him either.",
        "He held tight to the Lord and never stopped following Him. He kept the commandments the Lord had given Moses.",
        "The Lord was with him, and he succeeded in everything he set out to do. He rebelled against the king of Assyria and refused to serve him.",
        "He defeated the Philistines all the way to Gaza and its territory, from the smallest watchtower to the biggest walled city.",
        "In King Hezekiah’s fourth year, which was the seventh year of Hoshea son of Elah king of Israel, Shalmaneser king of Assyria came to attack Samaria and surrounded it with his army.",
        "After three years they captured it. Samaria fell in Hezekiah’s sixth year, which was the ninth year of Hoshea king of Israel.",
        "The king of Assyria carried the people of Israel away to Assyria. He settled them in Halah, in Habor by the river of Gozan, and in the cities of the Medes.",
        "This happened because they would not obey the voice of the Lord their God. They broke His covenant and everything Moses the Lord’s servant had commanded. They would not listen, and they would not obey.",
        "In King Hezekiah’s fourteenth year, Sennacherib king of Assyria attacked all the walled cities of Judah and captured them.",
        "So Hezekiah king of Judah sent this message to the king of Assyria at Lachish: “I have done wrong. Pull back from me, and I will pay whatever you demand.” The king of Assyria made Hezekiah king of Judah pay three hundred talents of silver and thirty talents of gold.",
        "Hezekiah gave him all the silver found in the Lord’s temple and in the treasure rooms of the king’s palace.",
        "At that time Hezekiah stripped off the gold that he, Hezekiah king of Judah, had used to cover the doors and doorposts of the Lord’s temple, and he gave it to the king of Assyria.",
        "The king of Assyria sent Tartan, Rabsaris, and Rab-shakeh (three of his top officials) with a large army from Lachish to King Hezekiah at Jerusalem. They marched up to Jerusalem and took their stand by the water channel of the upper pool, on the road to the Fuller’s Field (where cloth was washed).",
        "They called for the king. Eliakim son of Hilkiah, who was in charge of the palace, went out to them, along with Shebna the scribe and Joah son of Asaph, the royal record keeper.",
        "Rab-shakeh told them, “Tell Hezekiah that this is what the great king, the king of Assyria, says: What are you relying on that makes you so sure of yourself?”",
        "“You say you have a plan and the strength for war, but those are just empty words. Who are you counting on, that you dare to rebel against me?”",
        "“Look, you are leaning on Egypt, that cracked reed of a walking stick. If a man leans on it, it will jab into his hand and pierce it. That is what Pharaoh king of Egypt is like to everyone who trusts in him.”",
        "“But if you tell me, ‘We trust in the Lord our God,’ isn’t He the one whose high places and altars Hezekiah tore down? Didn’t Hezekiah tell Judah and Jerusalem, ‘You must worship in front of this altar in Jerusalem’?”",
        "“So now, please make a deal with my master the king of Assyria. I will give you two thousand horses, if you can find enough riders to put on them!”",
        "“How then can you drive back even one officer, one of the least of my master’s servants? Yet you count on Egypt for chariots and horsemen!”",
        "“And do you think I have come to attack this place and destroy it without the Lord? The Lord Himself told me, ‘Go up against this land and destroy it.’”",
        "Then Eliakim son of Hilkiah, Shebna, and Joah said to Rab-shakeh, “Please speak to your servants in the Syrian language (Aramaic), because we understand it. Don’t speak to us in the Jews’ language (Hebrew) where the people on the wall can hear.”",
        "But Rab-shakeh answered, “Did my master send me to say these things only to your master and to you? Didn’t he send me to the men sitting on the wall too? Like you, they are going to have to eat their own dung and drink their own urine.”",
        "Then Rab-shakeh stood up and shouted in the Jews’ language (Hebrew), “Listen to the message of the great king, the king of Assyria!”",
        "“This is what the king says: Don’t let Hezekiah fool you. He will not be able to rescue you from the king’s power.”",
        "“Don’t let Hezekiah get you to trust in the Lord by saying, ‘The Lord will surely save us. This city will not be handed over to the king of Assyria.’”",
        "“Don’t listen to Hezekiah. This is what the king of Assyria says: Make peace with me by giving me a gift, and come out to me. Then each of you can eat from your own grapevine and your own fig tree and drink water from your own cistern,”",
        "“until I come and take you away to a land like your own. It is a land of grain and new wine, of bread and vineyards, of olive oil and honey. Then you will live and not die. Don’t listen to Hezekiah when he tries to talk you into it by saying, ‘The Lord will save us.’”",
        "“Has the god of any nation ever rescued its land from the power of the king of Assyria?”",
        "“Where are the gods of Hamath and Arpad? Where are the gods of Sepharvaim, Hena, and Ivah? Did they rescue Samaria from my power?”",
        "“Which of all the gods of these countries has rescued its country from me? So how could the Lord rescue Jerusalem from me?”",
        "But the people kept quiet and did not answer him a word, because the king had commanded, “Don’t answer him.”",
        "Then Eliakim son of Hilkiah, the palace manager, Shebna the scribe, and Joah son of Asaph, the royal record keeper, went to Hezekiah with their clothes torn (a sign of grief). They told him what Rab-shakeh had said."
      ],
